\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
For a transcendental entire function $f$, write
$M(r)=\max_{|z|=r}|f(z)|$ and $m(r)=\min_{|z|=r}|f(z)|$; set $\log m(r)=-\infty$ if the circle meets a zero.
Its lower order is $\lambda(f)=\liminf_{r\to\infty}\log\log M(r)/\log r$, and its order uses $\limsup$ instead. For finite $\lambda$, define the optimal universal constant
\[
C(\lambda)=\inf_{\lambda(f)=\lambda}
\limsup_{r\to\infty}\frac{\log m(r)}{\log M(r)}.
\]
For functions of infinite order define analogously
\[
C_\infty=\inf_{\rho(f)=\infty}
\limsup_{r\to\infty}
\frac{\log m(r)}{\log M(r)\,\log\log\log M(r)}.
\]
All logarithms in the latter denominator are defined and the denominator is positive at sufficiently large radii. The problem asks for the exact value of $C_\infty$ and the sharp asymptotic behaviour of $C(\lambda)/\log\lambda$ as $\lambda\to\infty$, including whether this ratio has a limit. The infima express universal lower bounds, not bounds for a particular function. The source supplies nonmatching negative bounds and leaves the optimal constants undetermined.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Find the sharp large-growth constants controlling how small an entire function can remain on complete circles relative to its maximum modulus.

